  "course": {
    "narrative": "A 52-year-old female presented with progressive vision changes, dysarthria, headache, and incoordination starting after a motor vehicle accident. Evaluation revealed elevated CSF protein and T2 MRI signal abnormalities in the brainstem, cerebellar peduncles, and temporal lobes, suggestive of glioma. Multiple workups including lumbar punctures, cerebral angiogram, and a temporal artery biopsy were performed. A left temporal lobe brain biopsy showed atypical mononuclear cells and lymphocytes considered reactive. Her neurological status progressively worsened during her hospital stay, becoming very somnolent and difficult to arouse. She was made DNR at family request and transferred to a care facility requiring NGT feeding and 24-hour supportive care.",
